WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.840
My goal in listening is to make sure
I get that exposure to the language.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:08.580
Hi There, Steve Kaufmann here and today,
I'm going to talk about listening.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.500
This is going to be the third
in my series of my hierarchy

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.240
of goals in language learning.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.840
Remember, if you enjoy these
videos, please click on

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.310
the bell for notifications.

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:24.600
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:25.680
I do appreciate it.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.980
So you may remember that I said that
there areseven goals that I have

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.150
when it comes to language learning.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.760
And I kind of have a hierarchy of goals.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.890
My first goal is to acquire words.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:42.210
The number of words I know is the best
indicator of my level in the language.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.810
My second goal was to read a
lot and I measure, and I follow

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:50.790
this statistic we have at LingQ
on how many words I have read.

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:54.100
Now, the third goal is listening.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:00.644
It's, it's the third goal in the
hierarchy, but in many ways it's

00:01:00.644 --> 00:01:05.235
the most important because it's
the major activity that I do when

00:01:05.235 --> 00:01:08.804
it comes to language learning,
simply because it's so easy to do.

00:01:09.285 --> 00:01:13.815
So I can get up in the morning and
listen, I can listen while doing the

00:01:13.815 --> 00:01:15.735
dishes, I listen while I exercise.

00:01:15.735 --> 00:01:20.235
I listen while in the car,
the train or wherever I am.

00:01:20.235 --> 00:01:21.255
I can always listen.

00:01:21.464 --> 00:01:25.274
I can listen 15 minutes here, 20
minutes there before you know it, I

00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:27.995
have an hour of language learning done.

00:01:29.115 --> 00:01:34.185
The listening is how the brain gets
used to the sounds of the language, gets

00:01:34.185 --> 00:01:36.285
used to the structure of the language.

00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:40.304
We, as, as human beings, we
have been listening for a lot

00:01:40.304 --> 00:01:42.134
longer than we have been reading.

00:01:42.765 --> 00:01:47.505
And I think it's natural for the brain
to learn a language through listening.

00:01:47.744 --> 00:01:48.824
The reading helps.

00:01:48.945 --> 00:01:53.445
And I said that in my earlier video,
and I can reinforce that by saying,

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.759
in fact, if I look at my strategy and
listening, so I sort of have two types of

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:04.140
listening:  when I start in the language,
I need a lot of repetitive listening.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:08.690
That doesn't mean that I listen to say
one mini story 30 times at one sitting,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.450
but it means that I'll listen to that
story a couple of times, then I move to

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:17.100
the second story even while I don't really
understand the first story that well.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.785
Again, remember the brain wants novelty,
repetition and novelty as the great

00:02:22.785 --> 00:02:24.765
Manfred Spitzer said, we need them both.

00:02:24.945 --> 00:02:28.785
So I can repeat, I can listen to that
first story two or three times, but

00:02:28.785 --> 00:02:32.205
that is certain point even if I don't
understand it, I want something new.

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:36.015
And so typically I'll go lesson one a
few times, lesson two, lesson three,

00:02:36.015 --> 00:02:40.485
lesson four, back to lesson one, and
eventually I'll end up listening to that

00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:44.265
first story, 20, maybe 10, 20 times.

00:02:44.865 --> 00:02:46.845
In fact, it'll end up
being more than that.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:51.990
The mini stories are kind of the,
sort of the gym I like to call it.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:55.080
That's where I work on my core
capability in the language.

00:02:55.230 --> 00:03:00.030
So even much later in the language,
when I have acquired a lot more words, I

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:05.100
still like to go back to listening to the
mini stories, because I always discover

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:06.630
things that I hadn't noticed before.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.470
In other words, things that I'm
now open to because I've learned

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.610
other things in the language.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.780
So I start to notice them.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:18.375
Remember too, again, when it comes
to, because in, in, in lang...

00:03:18.405 --> 00:03:21.225
in listening, I don't really have a goal
"I want to listen to so many hours".

00:03:21.225 --> 00:03:22.065
I just want to listen.

00:03:22.185 --> 00:03:22.935
It's easy to do.

00:03:22.935 --> 00:03:23.445
I do it.

00:03:23.935 --> 00:03:27.375
Whether that's a hundred hours, 200 hours,
I don't pay the same amount of attention

00:03:27.375 --> 00:03:32.215
to the total number of hours I've listened
to as I do to the reading or the the

00:03:32.215 --> 00:03:38.834
sort of, uh, you know, LingQing and
adding to my known words total, because

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:40.394
the listening I'm just going to do it.

00:03:42.125 --> 00:03:47.805
And, um, but it's important to
realize that when you listen, it's

00:03:47.805 --> 00:03:51.285
not important or you shouldn't think
you need to understand everything,

00:03:51.285 --> 00:03:52.965
you don't even have to focus every...

00:03:53.055 --> 00:03:56.535
all the time when you're listening,
it's just getting the language in you.

00:03:57.255 --> 00:04:02.655
And so my goal in listening is to make
sure I get that exposure to the language.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:04.905
It's not to make sure
I understand anything.

00:04:05.205 --> 00:04:11.055
With enough listening the comprehension
will come, particularly if I am, if I

00:04:11.055 --> 00:04:14.205
have access, now, the mini stories are
relatively simple and there's a lot of

00:04:14.205 --> 00:04:18.015
repetition, but as you graduate from
the mini stories to more difficult

00:04:18.015 --> 00:04:20.085
content, you aren't listening as often.

00:04:20.204 --> 00:04:22.995
You're encountering more
words that you don't know.

00:04:23.415 --> 00:04:29.820
Uh, and so there, I think it's very
important to recognize it may be

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.580
frustrating that you don't understand and
make sure you have access to transcripts

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.650
because to listen to something that you
totally don't understand, I don't think

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.400
is very useful, but if you have access
to the transcript and you can look up the

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.710
words on LingQ say, and you listen again,
it may seem that every time you listen,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.750
you still don't understand the same parts.

00:04:49.229 --> 00:04:51.479
Uh, you have that experience all the time.

00:04:51.929 --> 00:04:56.280
However, if you keep going, you will
gradually understand more and more, and

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.479
I've had that experience many, many times.

00:04:59.979 --> 00:05:04.980
With more difficult content my strategy
typically is to listen, uh, and to, to

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.740
cover the material in sentence mode.

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:12.300
So that with timestamps, I can listen
to the sentence and even they're

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.220
the same words, the same phrase.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:15.000
I don't understand them.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:15.720
I can't pick them up.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.470
It goes too quickly.

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.790
It doesn't matter.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:21.810
Gradually you will understand
more and I've had that experience.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:30.585
So the goal in listening is to make sure
you listen enough that the brain will

00:05:30.585 --> 00:05:34.575
eventually get used to it and make sure
you have access to the transcript so that

00:05:34.575 --> 00:05:38.835
you can actually read it and have a chance
of understanding what you're listening to.

00:05:38.835 --> 00:05:42.465
You may still not understand it,
but you're gradually building

00:05:42.465 --> 00:05:45.585
up to that better level of
comprehension and listening.

00:05:45.585 --> 00:05:49.145
Comprehension is such an important
goal because it's the condition.

00:05:49.595 --> 00:05:52.455
Once you have that listening
comprehension, you can watch movies, you

00:05:52.455 --> 00:05:56.955
can listen to podcasts, you can engage
in meaningful conversation with people.

00:05:57.135 --> 00:06:01.995
So, and all of those activities, the
sort of higher level activities are going

00:06:01.995 --> 00:06:06.615
to improve your ability to speak, which
is where we eventually want to end up.

00:06:08.385 --> 00:06:11.594
That listening is such a key part.

00:06:11.594 --> 00:06:16.905
And in terms of a time, it's where
I spend most of my time between my

00:06:17.445 --> 00:06:21.615
different opportunities to listen during
the day I easily get in an hour and

00:06:21.615 --> 00:06:26.015
then I only need another half hour or
so of reading and LingQing if I'm using

00:06:26.015 --> 00:06:31.094
LingQ, uh, and I've got my hour and a
half a day of language learning time in.

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:34.424
So listening is the easiest thing to do.

00:06:35.205 --> 00:06:40.425
It's very important for forming
new habits in the brain for

00:06:40.455 --> 00:06:44.145
getting you to notice phrases that
you will eventually want to use.

00:06:44.205 --> 00:06:45.945
It's the best preparation for speaking.

00:06:46.635 --> 00:06:52.605
So while I don't track the number of
hours that I listen and, but I can leave

00:06:52.605 --> 00:06:58.845
you, uh, again, from my profile, just
in a couple of languages, you know, the

00:06:58.845 --> 00:07:03.335
extent to which my, my listening activity
has, you know, grown, progressively.

00:07:04.515 --> 00:07:06.315
But, uh, it's, it's so key to do it.

00:07:06.434 --> 00:07:10.544
And even if you stop for a while and
when you go back to listening and all

00:07:10.544 --> 00:07:15.015
of that listening activity that you have
done that has created new language habits

00:07:15.015 --> 00:07:16.484
in your brain, it'll still be there.

00:07:16.635 --> 00:07:21.075
You might have forgotten a few words,
but as you relearn these words, and

00:07:21.075 --> 00:07:24.135
as you listen to again, do it again,
you'll be back stronger than ever.

00:07:24.255 --> 00:07:28.844
So make sure you invest enough
time in your listening activities.

00:07:29.325 --> 00:07:36.570
And in the next video, I will talk about
how all of our input activity gradually

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.970
leads us to being able to speak well.

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:41.610
And what the goal, my goals
are when it comes to speaking.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.280
So thank you for listening.

